WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.320
The BRAF inhibitors work

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.640
by essentially targeting the mutant protein. When BRAF

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:09.960
is mutated, there are abnormalities in its DNA,

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.830
it produces an abnormal protein. And this
abnormal BRAF protein essentially

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.580
drives signalling in the cell,

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.820
much more than it would in the normal cell. What the BRAF inhibitor does is

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.320
it binds to that protein and essentially stops it signalling,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.300
and by stopping it signalling we essentially choke off the ability of the cancer cells to divide

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.600
and typically the cancer cell will then die.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.780
So certainly what we see in patients in
the clinic treated with BRAF inhibitors

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.780
is almost quite miraculous which is
that

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.980
cancer lumps that we can see under the
patient's skin can almost disappear

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.710
within 4-5 weeks. Something we've never
seen before from any of the cancers we treat.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:53.590
And when you look further by scans and
CT scans and what are called PET scans, and these are

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.590
hospital scans we do internally,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.580
you see, similarly, evidence of the
cancer disappearing

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.310
or shrinking in a very dramatic fashion.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.910
There is a twist in this tale which is that

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.580
so far what I've been telling you has been this almost miraculous benefit

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.119
from BRAF mutant melanoma patients
being treated with the right drug.

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:15.610
But unfortunately what we're finding is that within about a year to a year and a half

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.130
of starting treatment, and despite
remaining on this

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:22.930
BRAF inhibitor treatment, the cancer
comes back, and it comes back because it's

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:26.660
now resistant to the drug, it has developed resistance, and not

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.790
dissimilar to the resistance we see
with bacteria when we expose them to

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.600
antibiotics, if we use them for too long.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.060
What's quite clear is that that
resistance is because they've acquired

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.080
brand new mutations in other genes

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.000
and that means we will have to think of
second-line treatments to

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.549
overcome this problem. So there's a lot
of research now

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:48.450
in actually biopsying these tumours
when they become resistant and doing

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.620
exactly what we did previously,

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.720
which is we can sequence a tumour and ask what's changed at the DNA

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.049
level, what are the new mutations that may be the cause for this resistance,

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:00.520
and we can take those cells and keep
them alive and now

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.810
screen them with many many drugs, we will now find they are resistant to the BRAF inhibitor

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.780
but are they now sensitive to
something different

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.680
and can we now offer that to the patient in the clinic.

